.TH ST 1 st
.SH NAME
st \- simple terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B st
.RB [ \-aiv ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR opacity ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR class ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR geometry ]
.RB [ \-G
.IR geometry ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR line ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR iofile ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR title ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR line ]
.RB [ \-U
.IR opacity ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR windowid ]
.RB [[ \-e ]
.IR command
.RI [ arguments ...]]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B st
is a simple terminal emulator.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
disable alternate screens in terminal
.TP
.BI \-A " opacity"
defines the background opacity between 0.0 (transparent) to 1.0 (opaque).
.TP
.BI \-c " class"
defines the window class (default $TERM).
.TP
.BI \-d " path"
changes the working directory to
.IR path .
.TP
.BI \-f " font"
defines the
.I font
to use when st is run.
.TP
.BI \-g " geometry"
defines the X11 geometry string (in Text Cells).
The form is [=][<cols>{xX}<rows>][{+-}<xoffset>{+-}<yoffset>]. See
.BR XParseGeometry (3)
for further details.
.TP
.BI \-G " geometry"
defines the X11 geometry string (in Pixels).
The form is [=][<cols>{xX}<rows>][{+-}<xoffset>{+-}<yoffset>]. See
.BR XParseGeometry (3)
for further details.
.TP
.B \-i
will fixate the position given with the -g option.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
defines the window instance name (default $TERM).
.TP
.BI \-o " iofile"
writes all the I/O to
.I iofile.
This feature is useful when recording st sessions. A value of "-" means
standard output.
.TP
.BI \-T " title"
defines the window title (default 'st').
.TP
.BI \-t " title"
defines the window title (default 'st').
.TP
.BI \-U " opacity"
defines the background opacity of unfocused window between 0.0 (transparent) to 1.0 (opaque).
.TP
.BI \-w " windowid"
embeds st within the window identified by
.I windowid
.TP
.BI \-l " line"
use a tty
.I line
instead of a pseudo terminal.
.I line
should be a (pseudo-)serial device (e.g. /dev/ttyS0 on Linux for serial port
0).
When this flag is given
remaining arguments are used as flags for
.BR stty(1).
By default st initializes the serial line to 8 bits, no parity, 1 stop bit
and a 38400 baud rate. The speed is set by appending it as last argument
(e.g. 'st -l /dev/ttyS0 115200'). Arguments before the last one are
.BR stty(1)
flags. If you want to set odd parity on 115200 baud use for example 'st -l
/dev/ttyS0 parenb parodd 115200'. Set the number of bits by using for
example 'st -l /dev/ttyS0 cs7 115200'. See
.BR stty(1)
for more arguments and cases.
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.TP
.BI \-e " command " [ " arguments " "... ]"
st executes
.I command
instead of the shell.  If this is used it
.B must be the last option
on the command line, as in xterm / rxvt.
This option is only intended for compatibility,
and all the remaining arguments are used as a command
even without it.
.SH SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B Break
Send a break in the serial line.
Break key is obtained in PC keyboards
pressing at the same time control and pause.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Print Screen
Toggle if st should print to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Shift\-Print Screen
Print the full screen to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Print Screen
Print the selection to the
.I iofile.
.TP
.B Shift\-Up
Scrolls the terminal text layout up (backwards in history).
.TP
.B Shift\-Down
Scrolls the terminal text layout down (forwards in history).
.TP
.TP
.B Shift\-Page Up
Scrolls the terminal text layout up (10 rows backwards in history).
.TP
.B Shift\-Page Down
Scrolls the terminal text layout down (10 rows forwards in history).
.TP
.B Shift\-Home
Scrolls the terminal text layout to the first command.
.TP
.B Shift\-End
Scrolls the terminal text layout to the last command.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-Page Up
Increase font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-Page Down
Decrease font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-Home
Reset to default font size.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-y
Paste from primary selection (middle mouse button).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-c
Copy the selected text to the clipboard selection.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Shift\-v
Paste from the clipboard selection.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Alt\-Shift\-Page Up
Increase the background opacity level.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Alt\-Shift\-Page Down
Decrease the background opacity level.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Alt\-Shift\-Home
Reset to default opacity.
.SH XRESOURCES
.B st
checks the X server resource database for options on startup.
It reads the following resource names (prefixed by "st."):
.TP
.B font
Primary font string.
.TP
.B color0...color15
Interface colors
.TP
.B cursorColor, cursorColorReverse
Cursor colors
.TP
.B foreground, background, foregroundSelection, backgroundSelection
Interface colors
.TP
.B termname
Terminal type ($TERM)
.TP
.B shell
Default shell (e.g. /bin/sh)
.TP
.B minlatency
The minimum milliseconds st will wait before drawing to the screen after data arrives. Lower values reduce input lag but can cause text tearing during fast animations.
.TP
.B maxlatency
The maximum milliseconds st can delay drawing if text is continuously streaming in. This guarantees that even if a massive file is being dumped (e.g., cat huge.txt), the terminal will still force a screen refresh at least every 33ms (~30 frames per second) rather than locking up until the file finishes.
.TP
.B blinktimeout
The speed of the cursor/text blinking animation, measured in milliseconds. Setting it to 800 means a text attribute set to blink will cycle every 800ms. Setting this variable to 0 completely disables blinking across the terminal.
.TP
.B bellvolume
The loudness of the system bell audio alert. It accepts a value ranging from -100 to 100. Setting it to 0 entirely silences the terminal's physical beep.
.TP
.B tabspaces
The number of spaces a hard \t character occupies (usually set to 8).
.TP
.B borderpx
The internal padding/margin around the edges of the terminal window, measured in pixels. It stops text from pressing directly up against the outer window borders.
.TP
.B cwscale
Character width scale multiplier. By default, it is set to 1.0. Adjusting this values scales horizontal character spacing (kerning). For instance, setting it to 1.1 pushes letters slightly further apart to give text breathing room.
.TP
.B chscale
Character height scale multiplier. By default, it is set to 1.0. Adjusting this value alters vertical row spacing. This is especially useful if your chosen font misreports its height and leaves ugly gaps between lines or cuts off box-drawing characters.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
.B st
can be customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.
